Problems
G1-1: Managed vs Self-Hosted RAG Solution
Problem Statement:
 Should we use a fully managed RAG service or build our own self-hosted RAG solution?

Context:

Current infrastructure: AWS ap-south-1 (Mumbai)

Current LLM: Gemini 2.5 Flash via Google API

Use case: Retrieve relevant SOP sections to augment call analysis

Requirements: Multi-tenant isolation, vector search, document management

Constraints: Engineering resources, time-to-market, operational overhead

 

 

Options Considered
Option 1: Fully Managed RAG Service
Use a cloud provider's managed RAG solution that handles infrastructure, indexing, retrieval, and scaling automatically.

Candidates:

Vertex AI RAG Engine (Google Cloud)

AWS Bedrock Knowledge Bases (Amazon)

Azure AI Search (Microsoft)

OpenAI Assistants API with File Search (OpenAI)

Pros
Zero Infrastructure Management: No servers, databases, or vector stores to maintain

Auto-Scaling: Handles load spikes without manual intervention

Built-in Optimization: Query rewriting, parallel search, automatic reranking

Faster Time-to-Market: Reduces implementation complexity significantly

Enterprise Features: Security, compliance, encryption, access control included

Proven Reliability: Battle-tested infrastructure with SLA guarantees

Automatic Updates: Improvements to retrieval algorithms without code changes

Integrated Tooling: Monitoring, logging, debugging tools provided

Cons
Higher Recurring Cost: $100-300/month vs minimal self-hosted costs (which is elaborated in detail in the next section below)

Vendor Lock-in: Tightly coupled to specific cloud provider

Limited Customization: Cannot modify chunking strategies or embedding models in most cases

Regional Constraints: May require data storage in specific regions (compliance risk)

Black Box Retrieval: Less visibility into how retrieval decisions are made

API Dependencies: System unavailable if provider has outages

Pricing Uncertainty: Costs can increase with usage growth

Cost Component Breakdown
1. RagManagedDb (Spanner) - DOCUMENTED PRICING
Official Documentation:

"Basic tier offers a cost-effective and low-compute tier by setting the underlying Spanner instance to a fixed configuration of 100 processing units (0.1 nodes)" (Source: Understanding RagManagedDb)

"With a three-year committed-use-discount of 40% applied to a 100 PUs regional Spanner instance in us-central1, the monthly bill will be less than $40/month" (Source: Google Cloud Blog)

Pricing Tiers:



TierProcessing UnitsConfigurationMonthly Cost (List Price)With 3-Year CUD (40% off)Basic100 PUs (0.1 nodes)Fixed~$65/month~$39/monthScaled1000-10,000 PUs (1-10 nodes)Auto-scaling$650-6,500/month$390-3,900/month
Our Scenario: 100K queries/month, 100 tenants



Recommended Tier: Basic (100 PUs)
Reasoning: 
- 100K queries/month = ~3,300 queries/day
